        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sizing by CFM
          </h2>

          <p>
            Airflow, measured in cubic feet per minute, is the number that decides how much space a cooler can handle. Manufacturers translate it into square footage, but those figures assume a particular ceiling height, a set number of air changes and dry air. A larger, taller or leakier space needs more airflow than the label suggests.
          </p>

          <p>
            A practical way to check a claim is to work out the volume of your space. Multiply floor area by ceiling height to get cubic feet, and divide the cooler's CFM into it to see how many minutes it takes to move one room's worth of air. In dry climates, many guides suggest replacing the room's air every two to three minutes for a noticeable effect, which is why a 3,100 CFM cooler like the Hessaire MC37M is rated for far more space than a 525 CFM unit like the Honeywell CO30XE.
          </p>

          <p>
            Outdoors there is no room to fill, so think in terms of reach instead. A cooler blows a column of cooled air, and you feel it most within that stream. A larger, higher-CFM unit throws cool air further across a patio, which is why garage and workshop coolers like the Cool-Space 300 have big fans and high airflow figures.
          </p>

          <p>
            When in doubt, size up. A larger cooler on a lower speed is quieter and still effective, while an undersized cooler on its highest setting will be noisy and still struggle. Just remember that more airflow evaporates more water, so a bigger cooler also empties its tank faster.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Water, Tanks and Pads
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every evaporative cooler uses water continuously, and the reservoir size decides how often you refill it. Listings suggest the Hessaire MC37M's 10.3-gallon tank lasts three to four hours, the MC18M's 4.8-gallon tank about the same on its lower airflow, and the Cool-Space 200's 8-gallon tank up to three hours. Water use rises with airflow, heat and dryness, so treat these as rough guides.
          </p>

          <p>
            For anything bigger than a single-room cooler, a garden-hose connection with a float valve is the feature that makes ownership pleasant. The float valve keeps the reservoir topped up automatically, so the cooler can run all afternoon without attention. Check that the model you choose has one, as the Hessaire MC37M does per its listings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cooling pads, also called media, are where the evaporation happens. Thicker, rigid media generally cools more effectively and lasts longer than thin fiber pads, which is why Big Ass Fans highlights its 4-inch and 8-inch media. Pads gradually collect minerals from the water and lose effectiveness, so they are a consumable you will replace periodically.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hard water speeds up mineral build-up on pads and in the reservoir. If your water is hard, expect to clean and replace pads more often, and consider draining the reservoir between uses rather than letting water sit. Our guide to the <a href="/best-water-softener-system/">best water softener system</a> covers the wider effects of hard water around the home.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="indoor-outdoor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Indoor vs Outdoor Use
          </h2>

          <p>
            Evaporative coolers need a supply of fresh, dry air, and they add moisture to the space they cool. Outdoors that moisture simply disperses. Indoors it builds up unless there is somewhere for it to go, which is why manufacturers recommend opening a window or door on the far side of the room so humid air can escape.
          </p>

          <p>
            Portacool, for example, advises against using its coolers inside homes or closed spaces with no ventilation. The same principle applies to every brand: a sealed room with an evaporative cooler running will grow steadily more humid and less comfortable. Cross-ventilation is not optional.
          </p>

          <p>
            Garages and workshops are a good fit because they usually have a large door you can open partway, which gives the airflow a path through the space. That is the setting the Cool-Space 300 is designed for. Patios, porches and outdoor kitchens are also natural fits, especially in shade, where the cooler is not fighting direct sun.
          </p>

          <p>
            Indoors, small coolers like the Honeywell CO30XE and Hessaire MC18M suit a bedroom or office in a dry climate, as long as a window is open. If you have a whole-house fan, a cooler can work alongside it in the evening, and our guide to the <a href="/best-whole-house-fan/">best whole-house fan</a> explains how those systems move air through a home.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Running Cost
          </h2>

          <p>
            Low running cost is the main reason to choose an evaporative cooler over an air conditioner. A cooler only runs a fan and a small water pump, so its power draw is a fraction of a compressor's. The Hessaire MC18M is listed at about 85 watts, which at 17 cents per kilowatt-hour works out to roughly 1.5 cents per hour.
          </p>

          <p>
            By comparison, a typical portable or window air conditioner can draw several hundred to over a thousand watts, so it can cost ten times as much or more per hour to run. That gap is why evaporative cooling is so popular in the desert Southwest, where it works well and summers are long.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water is the other running cost. A cooler can use several gallons an hour on a hot, dry day, which adds up over a summer but is usually modest compared with the electricity saved. Replacement pads are an occasional cost, more frequent with hard water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Larger coolers use more power and water in proportion to their airflow, but they still cost far less to run than air conditioning a comparable space, especially a garage or patio that could never be sealed for an air conditioner in the first place.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Maintenance
          </h2>

          <p>
            Standing water and damp pads can grow mildew and bacteria, which is the main maintenance concern with any evaporative cooler. Drain the reservoir when the cooler will sit unused for more than a day or two, and let the pads dry out by running the fan with the pump off before you shut it down.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean the reservoir regularly to remove scale and sediment, following the manufacturer's instructions. A mild vinegar solution is a common approach for mineral deposits, but check the manual first, because some manufacturers specify particular cleaners or forbid others.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inspect the pads every few weeks during the season. Pads that are stiff with mineral deposits, discolored or giving off a musty smell should be cleaned or replaced, because they cool less effectively and can make the air smell stale.
          </p>

          <p>
            At the end of the season, drain the cooler completely, clean the reservoir, dry the pads and store the unit covered in a dry place. Doing this once a year prevents most of the problems people blame on the cooler itself, and it is a lot easier than scrubbing a reservoir that sat full all winter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When to Choose Something Else
          </h2>

          <p>
            If your summer afternoons are humid, an evaporative cooler will disappoint. In that case an air conditioner is the right tool for a room, a mini-split heat pump is the efficient choice for whole rooms you use daily, and a dehumidifier can help a garage or basement feel more comfortable. Our guide to the <a href="/best-mini-split-heat-pump/">best mini-split heat pump</a> covers the efficient end of that spectrum.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you mainly want a breeze, a good ceiling fan may be enough and costs even less to run than a cooler. Moving air makes a room feel cooler by helping sweat evaporate from your skin, even though it does not change the temperature. Our guide to the <a href="/best-ceiling-fans-with-lights/">best ceiling fans with lights</a> covers indoor options.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a garage in a humid region, consider a dehumidifier instead of a cooler, since lowering humidity makes heat more bearable and protects tools and stored items. Our guide to the <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-garage/">best dehumidifier for a garage</a> covers models that work in cold and hot garages alike.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you live somewhere dry and want to cool a whole house cheaply in the evenings, a whole-house fan pulls cool night air through the home, and our guide to <a href="/whole-house-fan-vs-attic-fan/">whole-house fans versus attic fans</a> explains the difference between the two. An evaporative cooler is then the daytime spot cooler for the room or patio you use most.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying one for a humid climate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Evaporative coolers depend on dry air. Hessaire says they work best at an average relative humidity of 40 percent or less. In humid regions they add moisture and barely cool. Check typical summer afternoon humidity where you live before buying.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running it in a closed room</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An evaporative cooler adds moisture to the air. In a sealed room that moisture builds up and comfort drops. Open a window or door on the far side of the space so humid air can escape and fresh, dry air can feed the cooler.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting square-footage claims literally</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Coverage figures assume dry air and an average ceiling height. A tall, open or leaky space needs more airflow. Work out your space's volume and compare it with the cooler's CFM, and size up when unsure.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving water sitting in the reservoir</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Standing water and damp pads grow mildew and bacteria. Drain the reservoir when the cooler will sit unused, run the fan to dry the pads before shutting down, and clean the reservoir regularly through the season.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the hose connection</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Refilling a large cooler by hand every few hours gets old quickly. For any mid-size or larger unit, choose a model with a garden-hose connection and float valve so it can run all afternoon without attention.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do evaporative coolers work in humid climates?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not well. They cool by evaporating water, which only happens readily in dry air. Hessaire says they are most effective at an average relative humidity of 40 percent or less, and other guidance says effectiveness drops sharply above about 60 percent. In humid regions an air conditioner or dehumidifier is the better choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much cooler can an evaporative cooler make the air?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on how dry the air is and how good the cooler's media is. In hot, dry conditions a good unit can lower the air it blows by 15 to 20 degrees or more. The Southwest Energy Efficiency Project notes that thick-media systems can reach about 93 percent effectiveness, while thin-pad designs reach 50 to 80 percent.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much water does an evaporative cooler use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Several gallons an hour on a hot, dry day for a mid-size unit, depending on airflow, temperature and humidity. Listings suggest a 10.3-gallon tank, like the Hessaire MC37M's, lasts about three to four hours. A garden-hose connection with a float valve lets the cooler refill automatically.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use an evaporative cooler indoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, in a dry climate, as long as the room is ventilated. Open a window or door on the far side so humid air can escape. Manufacturers such as Portacool advise against using coolers in closed spaces with no ventilation, because humidity builds up and cooling stops working.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an evaporative cooler cheaper to run than an air conditioner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, by a wide margin. A cooler runs only a fan and pump; the Hessaire MC18M is listed at about 85 watts, roughly 1.5 cents an hour at 17 cents per kWh. A window or portable air conditioner typically draws several hundred to over a thousand watts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I replace evaporative cooler pads?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                When they become stiff with mineral deposits, discolored or musty. That can be once a season or more often with hard water. Thicker rigid media, like the 4-inch and 8-inch media on Big Ass Fans coolers, generally lasts longer than thin fiber pads.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size evaporative cooler do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Start with airflow. Multiply floor area by ceiling height to get your space's volume, then compare it with the cooler's CFM. Many guides suggest replacing the air every two to three minutes in dry climates. When in doubt, choose the larger cooler and run it on a lower speed.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

